  </ctml:games>
  <ctml:notes>14th Norway Chess Women 2026 (Oslo, Norway, 25 May - 5 June 2026): a 6-player DOUBLE round robin over 10 rounds, 3 boards per round, under the Norway Chess scoring system. A decisive classical game (120 min for 40 moves, then 10 s/move) scores 3-0. A DRAWN classical game is followed immediately by an armageddon on the same board - White 10 minutes, Black 7 minutes with draw odds - worth 1.5 to its winner and 1 to its loser, so a drawn classical pairing yields 2.5 points between the two players rather than 1. Both cadences are one event here (header cadence "mixed"): participant/score carries the official Norway points and participant/placement the official finishing order; each participant's classical-only crosstable score and armageddon record are in its notes. Games are tagged by cadence - game/@round "1"-"10" classical, "A1"-"A10" armageddon, with the armageddon carrying the same board number as the drawn classical game it settled, plus game/timeControl - so both tables recompute from the 50 games. The builder asserts the official points, the games-played column, and the classical crosstable before emitting. Bibisara Assaubayeva led for most of the event and won on 16.5 points despite losing the final round to Ju Wenjun. Zhu Jiner finished half a point behind. The women's event carried the same prize fund as the open for the first time. Final standings: 1 Assaubayeva, Bibisara 16.5, 2 Zhu, Jiner 16, 3 Muzychuk, Anna 15, 4 Ju, Wenjun 13.5, 5 Divya Deshmukh 10, 6 Koneru, Humpy 9. The source numbers rounds 1-20 interleaved (odd classical, even armageddon); the split is cross-checked against TWIC's separately published classical and armageddon files, against the opening clocks (every armageddon game starts 600s/420s and no classical game does), and against the official points. The source TimeControl tag is unusable for this: 15 of the 20 armageddon games carry the classical tag, and those games' recorded time control here comes from their clocks. Movetext is the broadcast record, which runs longer than TWIC's website files in 2 game(s) and shorter in none. ECO codes are classified by longest UCI-prefix match (no ECO headers in the source). Every ply has a clock value. Identity is by FIDE id. The source PGN carries no federation, so each player's is resolved by FIDE id against the official FIDE standard rating list and cross-checked against the TWIC crosstable's federation column; the builder refuses to emit if the two disagree. No engine evaluations were present in the source, so none are invented. Uses the CTML 2.1 model (participant/placement).</ctml:notes>
